  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\n- Showers ending Sunday\n\nRegional radar shows a large area of showers over the cwa\nextending south to the Tennessee Valley. No big changes to the \nforecast planned for today. \n\nA slow moving low pressure system over central IL will continue \nto produce light to occasionally moderate rain today, tonight and \nending from northwest to southeast Sunday. Instability isn't high,\nbut there is enough SBCAPE to keep the mention of thunder in the \nforecast. Precipitable water values remain somewhat high...around \n1.75 inches, which suggests that a stronger shower or storm could \nproduce heavy rainfall; the far southern cwa remains in a slight \nrisk for excessive rainfall. Overall, basin QPF ranges from a \nthird to around an inch. Thus, while there may be localized \nflooding, widespread river flooding isn't expected. Heaviest \nrainfall will occur this afternoon as deformation forcing \ndevelops. \n\nThe upper trough axis will pass through the cwa Sunday morning, \nwhich should push the precipitation east; much of the cwa will be\ndry by Sunday afternoon.\n\n- Dry early next week, then chances for rain.\n\nHigh pressure will begin building into the region Sunday night and\ncontinue through Tuesday; we'll be dry through that time period.\nHighs will be in the mid 80s and lows in the mid to upper 50s;\ndewpoints will also be in the 50s. Thus, lower humidity is\nexpected early in the week.\n\nTroughing then develops late in the week and we could see several\nlow chances for additional precipitation.",
